7592186409899 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409899 1077 "field-3" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409899 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409895 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409899 1079 #uuid "9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409897 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409898 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409899 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1069 "q-181810" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1071 "![](images/59/592dc9d0d4713406cf9bf9c6ad966cb4446420113aa0e94fcfdcdb35b6471a9c.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$}$" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1072 #uuid "6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true] [17592186409900 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144}.\n$$" 13194139533585 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-53607ff6-5913-50ea-923c-e269930ccd31" 17592186409894 "answer-d74e258e-f0dd-579c-9148-3c83bfa32b0f" 17592186409895 "answer-d9099832-9536-5764-b8f9-82d9f50c96e0" 17592186409896 "answer-field-1979d639-7c7b-51e8-9e83-40721047de2a" 17592186409897 "answer-field-38cdacac-1881-5ec8-b9e9-d206d7013904" 17592186409898 "answer-field-9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" 17592186409899 "question-6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" 17592186409900}}
